=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — Office A users (PC1 at 10.1.0.100 and others) having
completely lost internet access — does not correspond to any fault present in
the live lab. A systematic end-to-end investigation was performed covering
every relevant subsystem:

R1's WAN uplinks are fully operational: both ISPA (203.0.113.1 via eth2) and
ISPB (203.0.113.5 via eth3) respond with zero packet loss from R1. R1 holds
a valid kernel default route (0.0.0.0/0 via 203.0.113.1/eth2) and is
redistributing it into OSPF via "default-information originate", which is
confirmed received at csw1, csw2, and dswa1.

NAT on R1 is correctly configured: MASQUERADE rules exist for 10.1.0.0/24,
10.2.0.0/24, and 10.6.0.0/24 on both eth2 and eth3, so Office A traffic is
properly translated to a public source address before reaching the ISPs.

The distribution layer (dswa1 and dswa2) has correct FRR OSPF configurations
using the proper interface names (br0.10, br0.20, br0.40, br0.99, eth5,
eth6), and both devices have the OSPF-learned default route installed.

Direct host-level verification: PC1 (10.1.0.100), pc2 (10.1.0.101), and
laptop1 (10.6.0.100) all successfully ping simulated internet IPs
(172.253.62.100 and 216.239.35.4) with zero packet loss and sub-2ms
latency. A traceroute from PC1 confirms the correct 4-hop path through
dswa2, csw2, and R1 to the internet destination.

Root cause: There is no fault — the network is fully healthy and the ticket's
premise is incorrect. No corrective action is required; the helpdesk report
appears to be a false alarm or describes a transient condition that has
already resolved.
=== END ===
```